<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Pope Leo XIII</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1810">1810</a>–<a href="/years/1903">1903</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a> · Also known as Vincenzo Gioacchino Pecci</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Pope Leo XIII (1810–1903), born Vincenzo Gioacchino Pecci, was head of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/catholic-church">Catholic Church</a> from 1878 to 1903. His encyclical Rerum novarum of 1891 laid the foundations of modern Catholic social teaching on labour and capital. He also acted as an international arbitrator, notably in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/caroline-islands">Caroline Islands</a> dispute of 1885 between <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spain">Spain</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/germany">Germany</a>, which he settled in Spain's favour.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/pope-leo-xiii.jpg" alt="Pope Leo XIII" width="320" height="427" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Braun et Compagnie · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Papa_Leone_XIII_(3x4_cropped).jpeg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Gioacchino Pecci was born on 2 March 1810 at Carpineto Romano, near <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a>, the sixth of seven children of Count Domenico Ludovico Pecci, a colonel in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/napoleon">Napoleon</a>'s French army, and Anna Francesca Prosperi-Buzzi. He studied at the Jesuit College in Viterbo until 1824. In 1837 Pope Gregory XVI appointed him a prelate, and he was ordained a priest on 31 December that year. He then served as papal legate to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/benevento">Benevento</a>, and in 1843, aged 33, as nuncio to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/belgium">Belgium</a>, where he was made an archbishop and developed excellent relations with the royal family.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>From 1846 Pecci was Archbishop of Perugia. In 1849 he called a provincial council to reform religious life, enlarged the seminary and hired Thomist professors and founded shelters and soup kitchens. He became a cardinal in the consistory of 19 December 1853. When <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pope-pius-ix">Pope Pius IX</a> died on 7 February 1878, the conclave assembled on 18 February, and Pecci was elected on the third ballot and took the name Leo XIII. He was crowned on 3 March 1878.</p><p>The Holy See had lost the Papal States, and under Leo it sought to regain international standing by acting as an arbiter of disputes. He opened the Vatican Archives to qualified researchers, among them the historian Ludwig von Pastor,. He created 147 cardinals in 27 consistories, among them John Henry Newman.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Works and ideas</h2><p>Leo wrote 86 encyclicals. In Aeterni Patris of 4 August 1879 he made the revival of the theology of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/thomas-aquinas">Thomas Aquinas</a> the charter of Catholic teaching, to be followed in the training of priests and in universities. Humanum genus of 1884 reaffirmed condemnations of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/freemasonry">Freemasonry</a>, arguing that its principles were incompatible with Catholic doctrine. He issued eleven encyclicals on the rosary and was called the Rosary Pope.</p><p>His encyclical Rerum novarum of 1891 addressed social inequality for the first time with papal authority, discussing the rights and duties of capital and labour. It affirmed workers' rights to a fair wage, safe conditions and trade unions, and the rights to property and free enterprise, and it rejected both atheistic <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/communism">communism</a> and laissez-faire <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/capitalism">capitalism</a>. It is thought to have laid the foundations of modern Catholic social teaching, and Leo was called the Pope of the Workers.</p><p>Leo XIII died on 20 July 1903, aged 93, of pneumonia followed by hemorrhagic pleurisy. He was buried in the Vatican Grottoes, and in 1924 his remains were moved to the Archbasilica of Saint John Lateran. He had the fourth-longest reign of any pope.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Pope Leo XIII” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Christian trade unions took their lead from <b>Pope Leo XIII</b>’s encyclical Rerum novarum (1891), which defended workers’ right to form associations and to receive a just wage, and the American Federation of Labor kept largely to bargaining over pay and hours.</blockquote><a href="/movement/labour-movement">Labour movement · Aims</a></li><li><blockquote><b>Pope Leo XIII</b> added it to the prayers said at the end of Low Mass; it is traditionally sung at the end of a priest's funeral Mass and said at the end of the Rosary.</blockquote><a href="/songs/salve-regina">Salve Regina</a></li><li><blockquote>A dispute between Spain and Germany over Yap in 1885 was settled by <b>Pope Leo XIII</b> in Spain's favour, and Spanish rule began in 1886.</blockquote><a href="/state/yapese-chiefdoms">Yapese chiefdoms</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in Carpineto Romano, in what is now <a href="/countries/italy">Italy</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=41.6,13.08&amp;year=1870" aria-label="Carpineto Romano on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="4869 681 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the birthplace of Pope Leo XIII"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5319 951h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5319 951h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=41.6,13.08&amp;year=1870">What was here in 1870? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
